theorem a-divisor-negating-a-quotient
  note        algebra divides by −(x/2), a quotient under a negation, from a
              line saying −(x/2) is not zero: x/2 is not zero because its
              negation is not.
  let x ∈ ℝ                                                           (H1)
  assume −(x/2) ≠ 0                                                   (H2)
  then x/(−(x/2)) = −2

1.  x/(−(x/2)) = −2
    algebra
    requires −(x/2) ≠ 0: from H2
